    lesson: 'The first two sessions were mechanism and rule, and a reader can hold both of those at arm’s length. This one cannot be held at arm’s length, because it is a report rather than a warning, and the people it happened to get to speak for themselves. It opens with a sound rather than a statistic. "And there was a great cry of the people and of their wives against their brethren the Jews." (Nehemiah 5:1) Stop on two words before going further. A CRY — the same word-world as the promise in Exodus 22:27, where Yahweh says that when the poor man cries out He hears him. And AGAINST THEIR BRETHREN. This is not a foreign creditor, not an occupying power, not an abstraction. This happened inside the covenant community, during a building project, under a governor who was himself lending, which is what makes the chapter a mirror rather than a case study about somebody else. Now take the three testimonies in the order the text gives them, because together they are a credit ladder with every rung named, and every rung is recognisable. THE FIRST RUNG, borrowing for what gets consumed: "For there were that said, We, our sons, and our daughters, are many: therefore we take up corn for them, that we may eat, and live." (Nehemiah 5:2) Note what they are borrowing for. Not luxury, not folly, not a bad habit. Food, for a large family, so they may eat and live. Nothing in that sentence is a moral failure, and that is exactly why this ladder catches people. THE SECOND RUNG, the productive asset pledged for the consumable: "Some also there were that said, We have mortgaged our lands, vineyards, and houses, that we might buy corn, because of the dearth." (Nehemiah 5:3) Say plainly what has happened. The thing that GROWS the food has become security for BUYING the food. Next year’s capacity is now underwriting this month’s meal. That is the rung that matters, and it is the one almost nobody notices they have stepped onto, because it feels like the same decision as the first one and it is not. THE THIRD RUNG, borrowing to meet a recurring obligation on the same land already pledged as collateral — the security a lender can take if the debt is not paid: "There were also that said, We have borrowed money for the king’s tribute, and that upon our lands and vineyards." (Nehemiah 5:4) The tax will come again next year. The land is already pledged. And then the terminal state, which should be read once without any commentary at all: "Yet now our flesh is as the flesh of our brethren, our children as their children: and, lo, we bring into bondage our sons and our daughters to be servants, and some of our daughters are brought unto bondage already: neither is it in our power to redeem them; for other men have our lands and vineyards." (Nehemiah 5:5) NEITHER IS IT IN OUR POWER TO REDEEM THEM. There is the bottom of the ladder, defined by the people standing on it. The power to redeem is gone not because they stopped caring but because the asset that would have funded the redemption is already held by somebody else. Every rung before this one had an exit. This one does not, and the reason it does not is arithmetic rather than character. Now the leadership response, which has three movements, and each one is a practice worth copying rather than merely admiring. FIRST, DELIBERATION. "Then I consulted with myself, and I rebuked the nobles, and the rulers, and said unto them, Ye exact usury, every one of his brother. And I set a great assembly against them." (Nehemiah 5:7) He consulted with himself before he spoke. That is the opposite of a reaction, and it is written into the verse so we would notice. And then he made it public — a great assembly — which is a judgement about the nature of the wrong rather than a loss of temper. SECOND, THE WITNESS. "Also I said, It is not good that ye do: ought ye not to walk in the fear of our God because of the reproach of the heathen our enemies?" (Nehemiah 5:9) His argument is not primarily economic and it is not primarily compassionate. It is that the nations are watching a covenant people do this to each other, and the name of Yahweh is attached to the outcome. THIRD, AND THIS IS THE HINGE, he indicts himself before anybody else. "I likewise, and my brethren, and my servants, might exact of them money and corn: I pray you, let us leave off this usury." (Nehemiah 5:10) He was in the practice. He says so out loud, in the assembly, before asking one other man to stop — and that is very probably the reason the room moved at all. A man who has not said that sentence about himself is asking others to go first. Then the remedy, which is where a great deal of modern teaching on this chapter goes soft and where the text does not. He does not ask for regret, or for a resolution, or for better intentions next season. He asks for RESTITUTION, itemised, with the deadline sitting inside the sentence: "Restore, I pray you, to them, even this day, their lands, their vineyards, their oliveyards, and their houses, also the hundredth part of the money, and of the corn, the wine, and the oil, that ye exact of them." (Nehemiah 5:11) EVEN THIS DAY. Not at harvest, not when it is convenient, not phased. And both halves — the assets back, AND the increase that had been taken. Then the instrument, because a promise with nothing behind it is how this chapter repeats itself in five years: "Then said they, We will restore them, and will require nothing of them; so will we do as thou sayest. Then I called the priests, and took an oath of them, that they should do according to this promise." (Nehemiah 5:12) And the sign, and the line that outranks everything else in the chapter: "Also I shook my lap, and said, So God shake out every man from his house, and from his labour, that performeth not this promise, even thus be he shaken out, and emptied. And all the congregation said, Amen, and praised the LORD. And the people did according to this promise." (Nehemiah 5:13) AND THE PEOPLE DID ACCORDING TO THIS PROMISE. The chapter ends on performance rather than on agreement, which is the difference between this and nearly every reform any of us has watched. So write your own three rungs this week, in the people’s own order, and be exact. What are you buying on credit that gets consumed. What have you put up as security in order to keep doing it. And what have you already lost that you cannot recover without help. Most households can answer the first honestly and get uncomfortable at the second. If the third is blank, this session is preventive and that is a mercy. And if it is not blank, read verse 11 again and notice two things about the remedy: it had a date on it, and the man who proposed it had already named himself in the wrong. Nobody in this chapter chose the third rung. They chose the first one, and the rest happened to them.',
